WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.585
Azure CLI commands run within a tenant,
subscription, region, resource group,

2
00:00:07.665 --> 00:00:09.886
and credential context.

3
00:00:09.966 --> 00:00:15.571
In automation, those values may come from
local account state,

4
00:00:15.651 --> 00:00:21.467
environment variables, CI/CD variables,
service connections,

5
00:00:21.547 --> 00:00:27.469
managed identity, workload identity, or
federated credentials.

6
00:00:27.549 --> 00:00:34.528
A secure script should not assume that the
currently selected subscription or

7
00:00:34.608 --> 00:00:40.001
default values are correct just because a
command would run.

8
00:00:40.081 --> 00:00:45.792
Target validation should happen before any
meaningful change.

9
00:00:45.872 --> 00:00:51.477
The script should confirm the expected
tenant, subscription,

10
00:00:51.557 --> 00:00:57.479
Azure cloud environment where relevant,
resource group, region,

11
00:00:57.559 --> 00:01:03.375
environment label, identity, and target
resource identifiers.

12
00:01:03.455 --> 00:01:10.857
A deployment script should make it
difficult to combine a production resource name

13
00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:18.234
with a development subscription, or a
staging identity with a production action.

14
00:01:18.314 --> 00:01:24.976
Development, staging, and production
should have separate assumptions,

15
00:01:25.056 --> 00:01:30.555
credentials, approval paths, and
monitoring expectations.

16
00:01:30.635 --> 00:01:34.548
A development helper may prioritize speed,

17
00:01:34.628 --> 00:01:41.184
while a production helper may require a
protected branch, change ticket,

18
00:01:41.264 --> 00:01:47.292
release window, operator confirmation, or
environment approval.

19
00:01:47.372 --> 00:01:55.092
The script should encode those differences
through validation and gating rather than

20
00:01:55.172 --> 00:01:57.815
relying on convention alone.

21
00:01:57.895 --> 00:02:03.288
CI/CD and environment variables should be
treated as input.

22
00:02:03.368 --> 00:02:10.771
They can be missing, stale, inherited, or
inconsistent with the requested action.

23
00:02:10.851 --> 00:02:15.715
If the tenant, subscription, resource
group, region,

24
00:02:15.795 --> 00:02:23.304
or resource target cannot be confirmed,
the script should stop with a clear message.

25
00:02:23.384 --> 00:02:29.940
The safest wrong-target change is one that
fails before it reaches Azure.
